  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Detroit manhandled the Yankees early and never let go. Cam Schlittler lasted four innings, surrendering six runs in the first three frames, and the lineup managed only four hits against Tigers pitching. Ben Rice hit a solo shot for show. The rest of the offense was noise. Eleven strikeouts, one run from runners in scoring position, nothing that mattered after Detroit's third-inning barrage made it 9-1.",
    "what_this_means": "This team has played for eight days and won twice. The pitching line looked fine on paper until the moment it mattered. Schlittler's season ERA was 2.08 before tonight, and it's not anymore. The offense is the real problem. They've scored three runs or fewer in five of their last eight games. That's not a slump. That's a door that won't open. Down 2.5 games in the division with a 48-37 record, they cannot afford nights where they show up for six innings and call it a start. The margin is disappearing because they're not scoring enough to survive it.",
    "what_to_watch": "Troy Melton starts for Detroit tonight at 1:35. He's been sharp this season with a 2.39 ERA, and the Yankees are not hitting well enough to punish mistakes.",
